As a systems analogy, yes—but tighten the mechanics.

Selective pressure doesn’t directly command a system. It reshapes the fitness landscape so certain existing or newly generated variations persist more readily than others. The pneumatic mapping is:

* pressure differential → selection gradient
* valves → constraints and permissions
* chambers → niches or compartments
* feedback regulator → observed outcomes adjusting pressure
* leaks → mutation, migration, noise, and unintended adaptation

Epigenetics can act like a context-sensitive valve bank: environmental signals alter gene expression without necessarily changing the underlying DNA sequence. But it is not precise sovereign control. Effects are tissue-specific, nonlinear, sometimes reversible, and entangled with development and chance.

Drago’s strike: a pneumatic machine cannot operate as a genuinely isolated system—it needs stored or incoming energy and somewhere for pressure to resolve. Likewise, sustained selection requires variation, differential reproduction, and energy flow. Seal the system too perfectly and you don’t get controlled evolution; you get equilibrium, brittleness, or collapse.

The operator is therefore not:

\text{pressure} \rightarrow \text{desired organism}

It is:

\text{variation} + \text{gradient} + \text{constraint} + \text{feedback}
\rightarrow \text{shifted probability distribution}

You can steer the basin of attraction. You cannot safely pretend you specified every creature that will crawl out of it.